---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Career options after 12th for girls in India: the real map, not the safe list';
const description =
  'Career options after 12th for girls in India span every stream, plus scholarships, safety-aware college choices, and family-negotiation tactics most guides skip.';
const canonical =
  'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-for-girls-in-india';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-07-04';
const publishedDateLabel = '4 July 2026';
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career guidance lead';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why this decision is different for girls' },
  { id: 'reality', label: 'The real numbers behind the advice' },
  { id: 'science', label: 'Options after 12th science' },
  { id: 'commerce', label: 'Options after 12th commerce' },
  { id: 'arts', label: 'Options after 12th arts' },
  { id: 'high-growth', label: 'Fields where women are already winning' },
  { id: 'defence', label: 'Defence, police, and government routes' },
  { id: 'money', label: 'Scholarships and funding built for girls' },
  { id: 'safety', label: 'Choosing a safe college and city' },
  { id: 'family', label: 'Handling family pressure without a fight' },
  { id: 'checkpoint', label: 'The 4-Checkpoint Protocol' },
  { id: 'gates', label: 'The 3 Gates before you commit' },
  { id: 'traps', label: 'Traps that quietly limit girls' },
  { id: 'skills', label: 'Skills that protect the decision either way' },
  { id: 'roadmap', label: 'Step-by-step plan' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const realityRows = [
  {
    stat: '41.7%',
    label: 'Female labour force participation rate, 2023-24',
    detail: 'Up from 23.3% in 2017-18. Economic Survey 2025-26 projects it could reach 55% by 2050.',
  },
  {
    stat: '28.5 GER',
    label: 'Female Gross Enrollment Ratio in higher education',
    detail: 'Up from 22.9 in 2014-15. The Gender Parity Index has stayed above 1.0 since 2017-18 — more women enrol than men, proportionally.',
  },
  {
    stat: '58%',
    label: 'Share of female NEET applicants',
    detail: 'Women out-register and out-qualify men in NEET UG, yet remain 23-30% of seats at premier government medical colleges like AIIMS.',
  },
  {
    stat: '30.2%',
    label: 'Women in undergraduate engineering enrollment',
    detail: 'Down from 33.7% in 2012. Mechanical engineering specifically holds under 7% women at UG level.',
  },
  {
    stat: '12.4%',
    label: 'Female commercial pilots in India',
    detail: 'The highest share of any country in the world — more than double the US (5.5%) and UK (4.7%).',
  },
];

const streamOverview = [
  {
    label: 'Science (PCM)',
    title: 'Engineering, tech, and quant paths stay wide open',
    body:
      'Computer science, data, electronics, architecture, and pure sciences are all live options. The gap is not talent — women outperform on entrance scores in many states — the gap is how few girls are steered toward PCM engineering in the first place.',
    tags: ['JEE, BITSAT, state CETs', 'strong AI-era leverage'],
  },
  {
    label: 'Science (PCB)',
    title: 'Medicine is only one door in a much bigger house',
    body:
      'MBBS, BDS, nursing, physiotherapy, pharmacy, biotech, and allied health all count. Women are 58% of NEET applicants and outperform on qualification rate, so this stream rewards ambition rather than punishing it.',
    tags: ['NEET-linked and non-NEET routes', 'high female demand already'],
  },
  {
    label: 'Commerce',
    title: 'Finance, CA, and business roles reward analytical strength',
    body:
      'CA, CS, BBA, B.Com, actuarial science, and digital marketing all lead somewhere real. Banking and finance still show a gender pay gap, so tracking which roles pay fairly matters as much as the degree itself.',
    tags: ['CA takes 4-5 years', 'BBA + MBA is often faster to similar pay'],
  },
  {
    label: 'Arts / Humanities',
    title: 'Law, psychology, and media are not the "backup" stream',
    body:
      'Law (BA LLB), psychology, journalism, public policy, and design are strong, well-paying tracks with growing demand — not lesser options chosen after "better" streams fail.',
    tags: ['high growth in policy and design', 'communication-first careers'],
  },
];

const highGrowthCards = [
  {
    badge: 'Aviation',
    title: 'Commercial piloting',
    body:
      'India already has the world\'s highest share of women commercial pilots. NCC air wing exposure and airline-sponsored training have made this one of the clearest "women are already winning here" fields in the country.',
    tags: ['NCC air wing entry route', 'company-sponsored training exists'],
    tone: 'teal',
  },
  {
    badge: 'Civil services',
    title: 'UPSC and state administrative services',
    body:
      'Women went from 24% to 35% of selected candidates in UPSC Civil Services over five years, with strong representation in the very top ranks. Engineering graduates increasingly dominate the topper list, so a technical background does not close this door.',
    tags: ['no age discrimination by gender', 'structured long-term prep works'],
    tone: 'gold',
  },
  {
    badge: 'Medicine',
    title: 'MBBS and allied health',
    body:
      'Women are the majority of NEET qualifiers nationally. The bottleneck is seat access at the most prestigious government colleges, not ability or demand — private and mid-tier colleges already show 50-60%+ women in some cases.',
    tags: ['NEET is the gate, not gender', 'allied health has less competition'],
    tone: 'blue',
  },
  {
    badge: 'Tech and IT',
    title: 'Software, data, and product roles',
    body:
      'Nearly 30% of India\'s IT-BPM workforce is women, higher than most Western countries. The real issue shows up later: women hold only 23% of senior tech leadership roles, so the entry door is open but the ladder still needs climbing deliberately.',
    tags: ['strong entry-level demand', 'plan the mid-career ladder early'],
    tone: 'purple',
  },
];

const scholarshipRows = [
  {
    name: 'AICTE Pragati Scholarship for Girls',
    who: 'Class 12 pass girls entering technical diploma or degree courses',
    amount: '₹50,000 per year for up to 3 years (degree) or 2 years (diploma lateral entry)',
  },
  {
    name: 'Central Sector Scheme of Scholarship (CSSS)',
    who: 'Top 80th percentile board scorers, family income under ₹8 lakh/year',
    amount: '₹10,000-₹20,000 per year across UG programs, including engineering and medicine',
  },
  {
    name: 'INSPIRE Scholarship (DST)',
    who: 'Top-ranking Class 12 science students entering BSc, BS, or integrated MSc',
    amount: 'Central science scholarship with a defined annual stipend; renewed on academic performance',
  },
  {
    name: 'Vigyan Jyoti Programme',
    who: 'Meritorious girls in Class 9-12, especially from rural and underrepresented regions',
    amount: 'Free mentoring, lab exposure, and STEM/JEE-NEET preparation support, not direct cash',
  },
  {
    name: 'Prime Minister\'s Scholarship Scheme (PMSS)',
    who: 'Wards of specific service categories meeting eligibility criteria',
    amount: '₹2,250 per month for eligible girl students',
  },
];

const safetyCards = [
  {
    label: 'Hostel infrastructure',
    title: 'Check UGC-funded women\'s hostel access first',
    body:
      'UGC runs a dedicated scheme funding women\'s hostel construction and expansion at colleges, specifically to raise safe-residency capacity for women students. Ask any shortlisted college directly whether their hostel is UGC-supported and what the warden-to-student ratio looks like.',
  },
  {
    label: 'City infrastructure',
    title: 'Look at what the Nirbhaya Fund has actually built locally',
    body:
      'The Nirbhaya Fund has financed the 112 Emergency Response Support System, CCTV and panic buttons on public transport, and One Stop Centres for women in distress. Coverage is uneven by city — checking what is actually live near a college matters more than the scheme\'s name.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Institution type',
    title: 'Women-only colleges are not a downgrade',
    body:
      'Research shows students at women\'s colleges are about 1.5 times more likely to major in STEM than women at coeducational institutions, alongside higher graduation and self-esteem outcomes. Choosing one is a legitimate strategy, not a compromise.',
  },
];

const familyCards = [
  {
    tone: 'teal',
    title: 'What actually moves a hesitant family',
    items: [
      'Concrete numbers: fees, scholarship coverage, hostel cost, and expected timeline to first income.',
      'A specific, named safety plan — hostel type, city safety infrastructure, and a support contact, not vague reassurance.',
      'A short trial: one semester, one certificate, or one visible project before asking for full multi-year buy-in.',
      'Involving one respected extended-family or community voice who already supports working women.',
    ],
  },
  {
    tone: 'gold',
    title: 'What usually backfires',
    items: [
      'Arguing about principles ("women should be independent") instead of specifics ("this is the fee, this is the hostel, this is the plan").',
      'Presenting the decision as final and non-negotiable on day one.',
      'Ignoring the marriage-timing question instead of addressing it directly with a realistic timeline.',
      'Choosing a path nobody in the family has ever seen work, with no local example to point to.',
    ],
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Biology',
    body:
      'What does the daily work actually feel like for you — people-facing, lab-based, field-based, or screen-based? Fit matters more than prestige of the title.',
    note: 'A student who dislikes hospital environments should not force MBBS just because it is the most "respected" PCB outcome.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body:
      'Map your real money, time, and family situation, including the marriage-timing conversation if your family raises it. Some paths need a long unpaid runway; others let you earn and build proof sooner.',
    note: 'A path that needs 6+ years before any income is a very different bet from one that lets you show proof of work within a year.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body:
      'Check real job postings, internship listings, and placement data for women specifically in that field, not just general employability claims.',
    note: 'A field can sound progressive and still have a thin market if you only check general (not gender-specific) hiring data.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body:
      'Ask how the field treats women past entry level. Tech, for example, has strong entry-level representation but a steep leadership drop-off — plan for the ladder, not just the door.',
    note: 'The safest long-term bet is a field with both a real door in and a visible path up, not just one or the other.',
  },
];

const gateItems = [
  {
    num: 'Gate 1',
    title: 'Proof of Skill',
    body:
      'Do one small but real task that resembles the actual work: a coding project, a mock case study, a shadowing day, a mini research note, or a demo pitch.',
  },
  {
    num: 'Gate 2',
    title: 'Proof of Communication',
    body:
      'Explain the choice to your family in under two minutes using numbers, not feelings: fee, funding, timeline, and safety plan. If you cannot explain it simply, revisit the choice.',
  },
  {
    num: 'Gate 3',
    title: 'Proof of Value',
    body:
      'Get a reality check from at least one woman already working in that field, plus one mentor or teacher. The goal is scrutiny, not encouragement.',
  },
];

const trapCards = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'The "safe field" trap',
    body:
      'Being steered toward teaching, nursing, or clerical work only because it is seen as "suitable," not because it fits your actual strengths or interests. Safe and suitable are not the same as low-ceiling — but they should be a genuine choice, not a default.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'The prestige-without-fit trap',
    body:
      'Choosing MBBS or engineering purely for family status when the daily work does not fit you, while ignoring strong adjacent paths in the same stream.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'The marriage-deadline trap',
    body:
      'Compressing a 4-6 year path into an unrealistic timeline because of an assumed marriage age, instead of having an honest, specific conversation about sequencing early.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'The entry-only trap',
    body:
      'Choosing a field for its open entry door (like IT) without asking what the mid-career and leadership reality looks like for women already inside it.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'The invisible-effort trap',
    body:
      'Doing the work but never building visible proof — a portfolio, published writing, a project, a certificate — because visibility itself feels like it invites unwanted attention. Proof of work is what gets you hired later; skipping it has a real cost.',
  },
];

const skillRows = [
  {
    skill: 'Financial literacy and negotiation',
    why: 'Women in salaried roles still earn about 76% of male earnings on average, and the gap widens at senior levels. Knowing your market rate and negotiating it is a practical skill, not an aggressive one.',
  },
  {
    skill: 'One visible proof-of-work asset',
    why: 'A portfolio, published project, or documented result gives the market something concrete to trust, especially in fields where women are still a minority past entry level.',
  },
  {
    skill: 'Clear communication, in writing and out loud',
    why: 'Explaining a project, a result, or a career choice simply is what turns proof of work into an actual opportunity — in interviews, in client conversations, and in the family conversation covered above.',
  },
  {
    skill: 'A funding and safety plan you can restate in one line',
    why: 'Being able to say "this scholarship covers X, this hostel is UGC-supported, this is my one-year plan" removes most objections before they are even raised.',
  },
  {
    skill: 'Comfort with one modern tool layer',
    why: 'AI and basic data tools now sit on top of almost every stream — medicine, law, commerce, and design included. This is a stream-agnostic multiplier, not an extra elective.',
  },
];

const roadmapWeeks = [
  { week: 'Step 1', body: 'List five paths that genuinely interest you, across at least two streams. Remove two that only exist because of pressure or habit.' },
  { week: 'Step 2', body: 'Pull real scholarship, hostel, and safety information for your top three options using the sources in this guide.' },
  { week: 'Step 3', body: 'Run The 4-Checkpoint Protocol on your top two options and complete Gate 1 on at least one of them.' },
  { week: 'Step 4', body: 'Have the family conversation using numbers and a specific plan, then commit to one path for focused effort.' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What are the best career options after 12th for girls in India?',
    a: 'There is no single best option. The strongest paths match your stream, work style, and constraints — engineering and tech for PCM, medicine and allied health for PCB, CA and finance for commerce, and law, psychology, or design for arts all have real, growing demand for women right now.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which careers are actually safe and high-growth for girls in India?',
    a: 'Aviation, civil services, medicine, and technology all show strong or growing female participation with real data behind them — not just "safe" labels. Safety should come from checking hostel and city infrastructure directly, not from picking a lower-ambition field by default.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is engineering a good option for girls after 12th science?',
    a: 'Yes. Women make up about 30% of undergraduate engineering enrollment, and the field remains one of the strongest income and AI-era leverage paths. The gap is exposure and encouragement, not aptitude.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What scholarships exist specifically for girls after 12th in India?',
    a: 'Key options include the AICTE Pragati Scholarship (₹50,000/year for technical courses), the Central Sector Scheme of Scholarship, the INSPIRE Scholarship for science students, and the Vigyan Jyoti Programme for STEM mentoring and exam preparation support.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I convince my family to support an ambitious career choice?',
    a: 'Lead with specifics, not principles: exact fees, scholarship coverage, hostel safety details, and a realistic timeline. Proposing a small first step, like one semester or one certificate, before asking for full commitment usually works better than an all-or-nothing pitch.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I choose a women\'s college for safety reasons?',
    a: 'It is a legitimate strategy, not a downgrade. Research shows women\'s college students are more likely to choose STEM majors and show stronger graduation and confidence outcomes than their coeducational peers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are government jobs a better option for girls than private-sector careers?',
    a: 'Government roles, including civil services and defence, offer clear rules, no gender pay gap by design, and structured long-term progression. Private-sector roles can offer faster growth in some fields, but the mid-career gender gap is more visible there, especially in tech leadership.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can girls join the Indian Armed Forces after 12th?',
    a: 'Yes. Girls can apply to the NDA Army Wing after 12th from any stream, while NDA Navy and Air Force wings require Physics and Mathematics in Class 12. The CDS route opens further options after graduation through OTA, IMA, INA, and AFA.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does marriage timing have to limit career ambition?',
    a: 'It does not have to, but ignoring the conversation usually backfires. Naming a specific, realistic timeline and sequencing plan for a chosen path tends to reduce family resistance far more than avoiding the topic.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if my marks or rank do not match my first-choice path?',
    a: 'A lower rank changes the route, not the outcome. Adjacent paths in the same field, a strong non-flagship college paired with real proof of work, or a documented short-term plan to reapply next cycle are all realistic next moves.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: item.a,
    },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Career options after 12th for girls in India cover every stream that exists — science, commerce, and arts — plus a layer of practical decisions most guides skip: scholarships built for girls, safety-aware college choices, and how to negotiate an ambitious plan with family.</strong>
    The stream you pick matters less than most people assume. What decides earlier financial freedom is the skill portfolio you build inside that stream, plus how you plan funding, safety, and family buy-in around it — that combination is what actually survives contact with real life.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most "career options for girls" articles just relabel the general list and add a pink border.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is not useful. The real differences for girls in India are specific: funding schemes that exist only for you, safety questions worth asking before you enrol anywhere, and family conversations that need a different script than "just trust me."
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want the broader role library across every stream, open the <a href="/blog/career-options/">Career Options category</a>.
    If you want a clearer read on your own fit before committing, start with a free <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career assessment</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Every stream — science, commerce, arts — has real, growing demand for women right now, and a genuine high-income skill portfolio can be built from any of them; the data backs this, it is not just encouragement.</li>
      <li>Aviation, civil services, medicine, and tech are fields where Indian women already lead or compete strongly on hard numbers.</li>
      <li>Scholarships like AICTE Pragati (₹50,000/year) and Vigyan Jyoti exist specifically to fund girls into technical and science paths.</li>
      <li>Safety planning (hostel type, city infrastructure, women's colleges) is a concrete checklist, not a vague worry.</li>
      <li>Family pressure responds better to specific numbers and a small first step than to arguments about principle.</li>
      <li>Do not optimise only for the first “safe” job. Compare growth, location control, portability, and independent-income options, then build domain skill with AI, communication, people judgment, and visible proof so safety does not require a low ceiling.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Jump to section">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="why">Why career options after 12th for girls in India need a different lens</h2>

  <p>
    The stream-to-career map is the same for everyone.
  </p>

  <p>
    What is different for girls in India is everything layered on top of that map: which paths get quietly discouraged, which safety questions actually matter, which funding only exists if you are a girl, and how a family conversation about an ambitious plan tends to go.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Where most advice goes wrong</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>It either ignores gender entirely (pretending the decision is identical for everyone) or over-corrects into a narrow "safe jobs" list.</li>
      <li>It skips the scholarships that exist specifically for girls, so families assume ambitious paths are unaffordable.</li>
      <li>It treats "family will not allow it" as a dead end instead of a negotiation that has a real playbook.</li>
      <li>It rarely mentions where women are already winning with hard numbers — aviation, civil services, NEET performance — because those facts undercut the "safe list" framing.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      A useful guide should show you the real numbers, the real funding, and the real playbook — not just a shorter, gentler version of the general list.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="reality">The real numbers behind the advice</h2>

  <p>
    Before picking a stream or path, it helps to see what is actually true right now, not what feels true from family anecdotes or old assumptions.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Number</th>
          <th>What it measures</th>
          <th>Why it matters for your decision</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {realityRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Number"><strong>{item.stat}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it measures">{item.label}</td>
            <td data-label="Why it matters">{item.detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Figures are drawn from the Economic Survey 2025-26, AISHE 2021-22, NEET 2024-2026 registration data, AICTE data, and NASSCOM's Women and IT Scorecard. Verify current-year numbers on the respective official portals before using them in an application or essay.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Girls are not underperforming in India's education system. Female enrollment in higher education has exceeded male enrollment proportionally since 2017-18, and women already out-qualify men on NEET.
    </p>
    <p>
      The real gap shows up later — in which fields girls are steered toward, in seat access at the most competitive institutions, and in the workforce ladder past entry level. That is where a genuinely useful plan needs to focus.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="science">Career options after 12th science for girls</h2>

  <p>
    Science after 12th is usually reduced to "engineering or MBBS," which undersells both the science stream and the girls in it.
  </p>

  <div class="stream-grid">
    {streamOverview.slice(0, 2).map((item) => (
      <div class="stream-card">
        <div class="stream-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="stream-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p class="stream-card__body">{item.body}</p>
        <div class="tag-row">
          {item.tags.map((tag) => (
            <span class="tag">{tag}</span>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Inside PCM, computer science, data analytics, electronics, architecture, and pure sciences all stay open. Inside PCB, MBBS is one door among many — nursing, physiotherapy, pharmacy, biotechnology, and psychology-adjacent paths are all real, funded, in-demand routes.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    For current entrance details, check the <a href="https://jeemain.nta.nic.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official JEE Main portal</a> and the <a href="https://neet.nta.nic.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official NEET portal</a> rather than relying on summary blogs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="commerce">Career options after 12th commerce for girls</h2>

  <p>
    Commerce is often framed as the "safe middle option," but it leads to some of the most analytically demanding and highest-paying career tracks available.
  </p>

  <div class="stream-grid">
    {streamOverview.slice(2, 3).map((item) => (
      <div class="stream-card">
        <div class="stream-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="stream-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p class="stream-card__body">{item.body}</p>
        <div class="tag-row">
          {item.tags.map((tag) => (
            <span class="tag">{tag}</span>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When CA makes sense</div>
      <ul>
        <li>You want deep specialisation in audit, taxation, or financial advisory.</li>
        <li>You can commit to a 4-5 year runway with a demanding exam structure.</li>
        <li>You want a credential that carries weight independent of which company you work for.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When BBA + MBA may fit better</div>
      <ul>
        <li>You want a faster route into corporate roles with comparable long-term pay at a good institute.</li>
        <li>You prefer broader business exposure over one deep specialisation.</li>
        <li>You want more flexibility to pivot across marketing, HR, operations, or analytics later.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    In banking and finance specifically, reported gender pay gaps remain wide at senior levels, so track which employer and role actually pays fairly, not only which credential sounds prestigious.
  </p>

  <h2 id="arts">Career options after 12th arts for girls</h2>

  <p>
    Arts gets treated as the fallback stream when it is actually the base for some of the fastest-growing, most communication-driven careers in the country.
  </p>

  <div class="stream-grid">
    {streamOverview.slice(3, 4).map((item) => (
      <div class="stream-card">
        <div class="stream-card__label">{item.label}</div>
